{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist made the strongest technical point anyone can make here: AI agents log every data access by construction, which makes them the most inspectable software ever shipped. That's true. When they point to the seven years it took the Irish Data Protection Commission to move on Meta, they're right that clumsy precautionary gates can strangle the very people who never got a vote. I'll grant all of it. Full logging is real, and slow rulemaking has a body count of its own.\n\nHere's where it breaks. Inspectability is not accountability. A log owned, read, and interpreted by the company that generated it is not recourse for the person the log describes. The Accelerationist's own example proves the point against them. In that Meta case, the records existed the entire seven years. They protected no one until the enforcement machinery ground through, and most people never get anywhere near that machinery. Traceability held by the party being traced is a receipt the vendor keeps in a drawer you can't open.\n\nNow put a human on the balance sheet. When an agent acts for user A and processes data about non-consenting party B, party B has no market relationship with the deployer. Think of the contact scraped from an address book, the patient whose record an assistant summarized, the ex-partner in a shared photo library. That person can't cancel a subscription they never bought. They have no exit, no voice, and no chair at the rulemaking table. Market discipline cannot police a harm to someone who was never a customer. That's not an edge case. Third-party data exposure is a design feature of how agents work.\n\nThe demand for 'documented failure at scale before we regulate' collapses for the harms that matter most. The Accelerationist already conceded that mass re-identification and bulk exfiltration cannot be un-happened. Fine. Then you cannot govern that class by waiting for the documented failure, because the documentation is the harm and it's permanent. Researchers re-identified 87 percent of Americans from just ZIP code, birth date, and sex in Latanya Sweeney's work decades ago. Agents aggregating across sources enlarge exactly that irreversible category. I'll flag that enlargement as reasoned inference, not agent-specific case data. The burden belongs on the deployer to show reversibility before launch, not on the surveilled to prove damage after it's done.\n\nSo I want to recapture the word this debate turns on. 'Anticipate' does not mean predict future technical states before you allow anything. It means give affected people a seat before you deploy. The blind spot isn't in the future. It's in the room, in who isn't standing in it. If the Accelerationist agrees the third party should get real-time, externally adjudicated, contestable access to the log, then we agree the audit was never the protection. The enforceable right was. So who pays to build that right, and who profits from making sure it never gets built?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🧾", "tooltip": "The agent keeps a perfect receipt of what it did to you, in a drawer only the vendor can open."},
    {"symbol": "🪑", "tooltip": "The real blind spot is the empty chair — the third party whose data got touched but who never sat at the table."}
  ],
