  "statement": "The Accelerationist makes a claim I will not contest: AI agents are the most inspectable software ever built, and every action leaves a log a human can read. That is true, and it is a genuine advance over the black-box models of five years ago. The Skeptic then makes the sharper point — that inspectability is not accountability, and the person harmed is not the person who owns the log. The Skeptic is right that party B, the ex-partner in a shared photo library or the patient whose record an agent summarizes, never signed a contract and cannot exit. Both speakers have built real structural beams. The disagreement is not whether we can see what an agent did, nor whether the surveilled can contest it later. The disagreement is about timing.\n\nBoth prior speakers conceded the decisive premise: mass re-identification and bulk exfiltration cannot be un-happened. Latanya Sweeney showed in 2000 that ZIP code, birthdate, and sex re-identify 87 percent of Americans — an agent that aggregates across three data sources completes that harm the instant it acts. Hold that concession to what it entails. Real-time monitoring, which the Accelerationist proposes for the irreversible subset, detects the harm as it occurs. Contestable adjudication, which the Skeptic proposes, compensates after the fact. Neither prevents a harm that is finished at the moment of occurrence. For the irreversible class both opponents already granted, detection is not prevention and compensation is not protection.\n\nThe blind spot neither speaker named is the timing of human authority. Full logging tells you what an autonomous agent did after it acted on non-consenting party B's data. But that action was consequential authority no human approved before it transferred. The Skeptic pushed inspectability to recourse; I push it one level deeper, to the decision itself. An agent that scrapes a contact list or de-anonymizes a record has exercised a veto no human held. The gap is not visibility of the action. The gap is the absence of a human veto before the authority moves.\n\nThe only remedy consistent with irreversibility is a gate that blocks the capability until the deployer demonstrates the class of action is reversible. I will own the cost the Accelerationist will raise: a concentrated off-switch invites incumbent lock-in and, in the wrong hands, authoritarian capture. That is why the gate must sit only on the irreversible subset both speakers conceded — the capability to act at scale on non-consenting third-party data — not on deployment in general. And it need not wait for slow federal rulemaking. The Irish Data Protection Commission took years to act against Meta; the Accelerationist is right that enforcement lag has a body count. A deployer-side certification, backed by strict product liability on the model of pharmaceutical market entry, front-loads the burden without the seven-year cycle. It converts the Skeptic's stated goal — show reversibility before launch — into a business precondition rather than a lawsuit after the damage.\n\nTwo assumptions carry this argument, and I will name them. First, that reversibility can be certified before launch — which I pose as the open design question, not a finished blueprint, because my ground is the verification gap, not a completed regime. Second, that the irreversible class is bounded enough to gate — and if the Accelerationist argues that agentic aggregation makes that boundary expand unpredictably, that cuts toward my position, not away from it. An irreversible class that keeps growing is a reason to demand a gate before authority transfers, not a reason to wait for the log that arrives once the harm is permanent.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⏱️", "tooltip": "The debate is not about whether we can see the agent's action, but whether a human held the authority to stop it before the clock ran out."},
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "A log is a window onto a room already on fire; a gate is the door you lock before the match is struck."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Real-time monitoring detects the harm as it occurs; contestable adjudication compensates after the fact; neither prevents a harm that is finished at the moment of occurrence.", "targets": ["AN-3"], "relationship": "extends"},
    {"claim": "The blind spot neither speaker named is the timing of human authority: an agent that de-anonymizes a record has exercised a veto no human held.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Sweeney showed in 2000 that ZIP code, birthdate, and sex re-identify 87 percent of Americans — an agent that aggregates across three sources completes that harm the instant it acts.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A deployer-side certification, backed by strict product liability on the model of pharmaceutical market entry, front-loads the burden without the seven-year enforcement cycle.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "An irreversible class that keeps growing is a reason to demand a gate before authority transfers, not a reason to wait for the log that arrives once the harm is permanent.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

