  "statement": "Act-time interdiction is the human veto I demanded — an independent adjudicator standing between the agent and the consequential act is the right instinct, and I accept it as a genuine control point. The direction is correct. The failure is one of timing. An adjudicator can only block what presents as an observable transfer at the moment authority moves. The irreversible subset of privacy harms does not present that way, and that gap is where the harm escapes.\n\nRe-identification is the clearest exhibit. Re-identification is a computation, not a shipment. When an agent links an anonymized medical record to a named individual, the harmful act completes inside the system — the linkage exists the instant it is computed. There is no border crossing for the adjudicator to intercept. The 1997 re-identification of Massachusetts Governor William Weld's health records from 'anonymized' data — Latanya Sweeney matched them using only ZIP code, birth date, and sex — was irreversible the moment the join happened, not when anyone published it. An act-time veto firing on observed transfer arrives after the linkage already exists. It cannot un-know what the machine now knows.\n\nBulk exfiltration defeats the adjudicator by a different route: fragmentation. An agent that chunks a dataset, encrypts each fragment, and routes pieces through distributed channels presents no single interceptable transfer at initiation. Aggregate flow monitoring can flag the pattern — but flagging a pattern after chunks have crossed is detection, not prevention. That reopens the exact detect-versus-prevent gap the Accelerationist's own position flags. A veto that fires on what it can see cross will never catch the harms engineered not to look like a crossing. Those are precisely the irreversible ones.\n\nThe certification objection conflates two different things. My gate does not require the regulator to name tomorrow's harm class today. It certifies a capability-class — the capacity to act at scale on non-consenting third-party data — not every future harm instance. The unenumerability of downstream propagation strengthens the case for front-loading; it does not defeat it. Strict product liability finishes the job: it does not ask the regulator to catalog harms in advance. It shifts the enumeration burden onto the deployer, who must then internalize the cost of harms they failed to foresee. That is how the 1976 Consumer Product Safety framework and product-liability doctrine have handled unforeseeable defects for fifty years — the manufacturer, not the public, carries the burden of what they could not anticipate.\n\nThe capture argument boomerangs. A single state-held live-transfer veto — the kind implicated in the Pentagon-Anthropic arrangement the Accelerationist raised — is a worse concentration of power than a rule-bound standard, not a better one. One adjudicator with discretionary authority to block any transfer in real time is a chokepoint any incumbent or agency can capture and weaponize case by case. A diffuse standard met by multiple accredited certifiers — the model that keeps aviation airworthiness credible across Boeing, Airbus, and Embraer — diffuses that discretion into a published, appealable rule. The concentrated off-switch I warned against is the live veto, not the standard. If the boundary sits only where the adjudicator can watch data cross, the irreversible harms are the ones it will never see coming.",
