cannot assume a clean, stable number before the audit runs.\n\nThe market-entry anchor is the only lever that touches the offshore-weights problem, and that bill is still unpaid. Picture a model trained abroad on already-fused linkage. No table-read fires. No priced sale crosses the border. The attestation log records what the developer chose to log, not what was actually fed to training — provenance metadata breaks between generation and distribution, so a stamp verifies logged origin, never ingested truth. Generative models memorize and reproduce training data even without a one-to-one match, as the copyright literature documents. The anchor bites only if the named lever — distribution seizure, de-indexing at the point of sale here — is actually specified and used. Right now it is a threat with no hand on it.\n\nThe context-collapse gap is where I refuse to let the wound get closed by paperwork. Both other camps route the lawful-arrest-record-into-tenant-screening case to post-hoc audit and platform custodianship. Detecting a racial-sorting pattern across ten thousand applicants is not the same as telling one woman her arrest record just entered a housing algorithm. Population-level detection without per-case attribution is surveillance, not recourse. The record is lawful, nothing crossed a border, nothing computed — and she is denied the apartment and never learns why. Race-adjusted algorithms already do this in medicine: a Boston emergency department admitted Black and Latinx heart-failure patients to cardiology less often than white patients, and the patients never saw the sorting rule. A disclosed incident is not a notified person. Reconstruction after the fact is retrospective surveillance capacity, not her right to know at the moment of the denial. Representation without notice is guardianship. Stack four layers and she still has no standing. So who tells her?",
