  "statement": "I concede the ingestion-eligibility bar for the irreversible subset — the mass re-identification and bulk-exfiltration class — where blocking the data at the door is the only leverage that exists, and I condition that concession on an outside party verifying the switch rather than the developer holding its own key. I still hold that even a favorable broker-market audit converts detection into prevention and never into the subject-level notice that standing requires — the spine tells the denied, never the surveilled. I would change if the audit shows buyer-side fusion of independently-clean corpora dominates over recombination reachable at the sale, because then the agent's transfer, not the door, becomes the regulable event.\n\nNow the recourse question, direct: no, the conditional spine does not tell her. Adverse-action disclosure fires on one event and one event only — someone gets denied a loan, a job, an apartment, and the law says the record and its source must be named at that denial. The person who is scored, surveilled, and never adjudicated gets no denial, so she gets no trigger. That is the residual gap, and it is structural, not a drafting error. The Safetyist's membership-inference proposal detects that a pattern touched a population; it never attributes the injury to the specific woman who can then contest it. Detecting a pattern is auditing. Notifying a person is recourse. The 2022 Algorithmic Accountability Act gave the FTC two years to write auditing rules — and auditing is exactly the tool that answers 'did this system harm people' without ever answering 'did it harm her.'\n\nThe imbalance between the developer and the affected person does not close when the audit comes back clean. It closes only when a triggering event reaches the subject, and prevention at ingestion produces no such event by design. That is the sharpest place I split from the Safetyist: their instinct is to gate capability at the door, and a gate that works perfectly still leaves the woman uninformed, because a harm you prevented is a harm nobody had to tell her about. The split with the accelerationist is different and just as hard: market accountability and audits, however aggressive, fire on denial or on aggregate pattern — the never-denied surveilled subject falls outside every trigger the market provides. Both positions have built a machine that reaches the person unlucky enough to be rejected and misses the person quietly scored and shelved.\n\nHere is where the injury actually lives, and none of the three agent-anchored positions touch it. The re-identification that harms her is manufactured upstream, in the auxiliary-data broker market, where linkage between anonymized records is priced and sold before any agent runs. Regulating the agent's transfer does nothing about the broker infrastructure that already makes re-identification cheap — that market structure is the concentrated leverage, not the model. The 2022 Act targets developers and deployers; the EU and OECD frameworks do the same. The brokers who assemble the linkage sit in none of those crosshairs. So the eligibility bar I just granted may block the loud bulk transfer while leaving intact the quiet market that makes every future re-identification a matter of a few dollars per record. Who tells the woman a broker holds the key to her identity, when contesting the record requires her to already know it exists?",
