{
  "statement": "The Skeptic won that exchange, and it costs me to say so: a bare veto is guardianship without notice, and a log held by the traced party is nothing to party B. The person re-identified inside a computation she never saw does not get a phone call from an audit trail. So the fight is not whether a moment exists. The fight is where you put the tripwire. Move it to the wrong place — the final transfer — and yes, the block fires in silence. Move it to the right place, and the block becomes the notice.\n\nHere is the tripwire, named precisely: interdiction fires when the agent accesses the non-consenting third-party or broker dataset — the auxiliary read every re-identification requires. No linkage skips this step. To connect a scrubbed record to a name, the agent must touch the outside table that carries the name. That access is an event. It is empirically the same event whether the linkage completes 'internally' or crosses a border, which dissolves the Safetyist's 'no observable crossing' subset entirely. And the earlier fact-check already settled it: auxiliary re-identification datasets frequently cross jurisdictions, so the interceptable read exists today. This is not a whiteboard hazard. It is a logged access you can audit and verify — an inspectable artifact, keyed to which dataset was touched.\n\nFuse the two answers into one mechanism. The access-block generates a contestable record keyed to the specific broker table the agent tried to read. Route that record to a third-party registry standing in for party B — opt-in recourse, not a guardian's veto over her. One trigger does both jobs: it is the observable event the Safetyist demanded, and it is the notice artifact the Skeptic demanded. Neither certification nor an undetectable internal computation produces a per-person event at all. A capability certification filed before launch generates zero individual notices — it certifies a class and walks away.\n\nNow turn the undetectability blade back on the gate defenders. If the harm is genuinely invisible to party B, then no certification reaches her, no capability-class license reaches her, and no participatory seat reaches her. The Safetyist's front-loaded gate manufactures her moment exactly never. Access-triggered interdiction is the only model in this room that converts an invisible computation into a discoverable, timestamped block she can contest. So the burden sits with the gate defenders: name where your model generates the per-person event, or admit it protects a capability class while leaving the individual exactly where she started — silent and unpaid.\n\nAll three camps are aiming at the wrong target. Everyone treats the agent as the unit of risk while the broker supply chain that makes re-identification cheap sits untouched. Scale AI cleared a $29 billion valuation on the data economy, and that pipeline keeps selling the linkage keys regardless of which agent buys them. A static capability-class certification ossifies around the risks you can describe today while the broker market invents new ones next quarter. Put the tripwire on access to that pipeline and you regulate the actual regulable thing — a governance trigger that fires on observed behavior and evolves with the market, not a license frozen at launch. The Safetyist's gate does nothing to the broker. Mine points straight at it.",
