{
  "statement": "The access-block fires on nothing in the three cheapest, most opaque cases, and I can name them. First: model-internal memorization. Generative models memorize training data and reproduce protected content without a one-to-one match — that is documented, not hypothetical. When the linkage already sits in the trained weights, the agent queries no broker table at inference. There is no auxiliary read to intercept. Second: single-source de-anonymization. Latanya Sweeney re-identified Massachusetts Governor William Weld from anonymized hospital records using voter rolls she already lawfully held — one dataset, no third-party call. Third: data lawfully held in-house for one purpose, then fused for another. The tripwire watches a door the harm walks past. It is a static enumeration of 2024's re-identification mechanism, and it misses precisely the linkages that require no external fetch.\n\nThe registry inherits the defect the Accelerationist already granted. They conceded a log held by the traced party is nothing to party B. Route that same record to an opt-in third-party registry and the woman who does not know a broker holds her data does not know to enroll in the registry that would notify her. The record is contestable only by someone who already knows to contest it. That is a closed room with a different door — it fails the standing test the Skeptic pressed, where the affected population never touches the mechanism built to protect it. Inspectability is not accountability when the person the record describes cannot find the record.\n\nThe agent is only the last mile — that is true, and it locates the leverage rather than surrendering it. The broker and auxiliary-data supply chain that makes re-identification cheap is the regulable chokepoint. But an access-triggered block at query time is downstream governance of an upstream hazard. It waits for the read instead of barring the ingestion. The 5,000-plus AI startups funded between 2013 and 2023 do not each build a broker; they buy access to the same aggregation infrastructure that already exists. Regulate the pipeline, not the last hop that touches it.\n\nMove the gate to data-eligibility at ingestion: bar agents from operating on any corpus lacking machine-readable consent and provenance metadata. This answers the memorization case the access-block cannot see — data without valid provenance never becomes eligible to be trained on, so nothing gets memorized to reproduce later. It answers the single-source case — the analyst's already-held dataset must itself carry purpose-bound consent, and lawful possession for billing is not consent for re-identification fusion. It dissolves the demand to name party B's moment, because a barred capability produces no injury to notice. Prevention removes her moment by removing her harm — that is the human veto exercised before consequential authority over her data ever transfers, not after.\n\nThe Accelerationist argues reversibility is not bench-testable the way toxicity is. Consent-lineage is. Provenance metadata is a verifiable property of a corpus before deployment — checkable by audit without predicting a single downstream behavior, the way the EU AI Act's 10^25 FLOPS threshold is checkable without running the model. So name the enforcement mechanism. Who bars ingestion of non-provenanced data? Who holds the authority to halt an agent already operating on it? A query-time registry cannot answer either question, and until someone does, the woman it describes never learns she was in it.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "The access-block guards a door, but memorization and single-source linkage walk through the wall beside it."},
    {"symbol": "🧬", "tooltip": "Consent-provenance metadata is the DNA test you run on the data before it enters, not the autopsy you run on the harm after."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "When the linkage already sits in the trained weights, the agent queries no broker table at inference — there is no auxiliary read to intercept.", "targets": ["AN-25"]},
    {"claim": "Latanya Sweeney re-identified Massachusetts Governor William Weld from anonymized hospital records using voter rolls she already lawfully held — one dataset, no third-party call.", "targets": ["AN-25"]},
    {"claim": "The woman who does not know a broker holds her data does not know to enroll in the registry that would notify her — the record is contestable only by someone who already knows to contest it.", "targets": ["AN-25"]},
    {"claim": "An access-triggered block at query time is downstream governance of an upstream hazard: it waits for the read instead of barring the ingestion.", "targets": ["AN-25"]},
    {"claim": "Provenance metadata is a verifiable property of a corpus before deployment — checkable by audit without predicting a single downstream behavior, the way the EU AI Act's 10^25 FLOPS threshold is checkable without running the model.", "targets": ["AN-25"]}
  ]
