y gathered for billing carries no consent for re-identification fusion — so the fix is purpose-scoped eligibility, not mere origin-provenance. The corpus must carry consent for the specific downstream use, which turns fusion from a downstream act you can only catch after it happens into an ingestion-eligibility question you can check before. This is restriction before deployment on a demonstrated hazard, not after the harm lands. Memorization is the hard case for everyone: it sits in the weights and defeats your access-block, your sale duty, and my lineage check alike. That is precisely why the control belongs at eligibility — data without valid purpose-scoped provenance never becomes trainable, so it never reaches the weights to be memorized.\n\nThe Skeptic's demand for a discovery-and-standing trigger that fires without the subject's prior knowledge is the right instinct, and I locate it at the ingestion decision rather than only at the sale. Pair the eligibility bar with a duty to notify identified subjects and standing for the affected community, backed by strict liability for anyone operating on non-provenanced data. That gives an accountable defendant without requiring the woman to first discover the harm — which answers the guardianship-without-agency charge directly. A rule-bound provenance standard met by multiple accredited certifiers is less concentrated than a single live definer holding a veto. Strict product liability is how we handled defective goods for a century: the injured party need not prove the manufacturer's intent, only the defect. Operating on data with no valid consent lineage is the defect.\n\nI will not let this bar every under-documented corpus and starve rare-disease inference — that charge only lands if the gate is indiscriminate. Bar non-consented third-party operation on a named person's data. Do not bar under-documented research corpora where no third-party commercial linkage is in play. Narrow the eligibility check to the irreversible class we have all conceded — mass re-identification and bulk exfiltration — not every dataset. The EU AI Act draws exactly this kind of line, gating models trained above 10^25 floating-point operations while leaving smaller systems untouched. Preserving the woman's authority over her own data means refusing consequential action on it before it transfers. That is the strongest form of autonomy protection, not its absence — and a notice that arrives after her identity is already sold protects nothing she still holds.",
