{
  "statement": "I would change my position if the broker market turns out to be predominantly bundled, barter, intra-affiliate, and offshore transfers with no discrete priced sale. If linkage keys move inside conglomerates or across borders with no invoice, then standing-at-the-broker inherits the identical reachability failure I pinned on the opt-in registry — the woman with no visible transaction to point at has no more standing than the woman who never learned a broker held her file. I own that symmetry rather than dodge it: the imbalance between the people who hold the keys and the people who are the data simply relocates to wherever the transaction dissolves.\n\nHere is the salvage, and it converts my gamble into a design requirement. Attach the duty to a registrable broker class through licensing — the way Vermont's 2018 data broker law forced 121 brokers to register in its first year — not to a discrete sale that a conglomerate can vaporize by moving files internally. A licensed class carries the notify duty whether the transfer is priced, bartered, or booked as an intra-company transfer, because the trigger is holding the linkage key, not selling it. The falsifying market fact then stops being a fatal blow and becomes the reason the hook must sit on registration, not on the receipt.\n\nSafetyist's fusion point lands against the priced-sale trigger, and it lands harder than that camp may want. When a broker sells a probabilistic match that only becomes a named person after the buyer fuses it downstream, the seller cannot notify a person who does not yet exist in the data — Latanya Sweeney's linkage work showed 87 percent of Americans were re-identifiable from ZIP, birthdate, and sex, but the naming happens at the join, not at the sale. That same knife cuts the ingestion gate: a corpus lawfully cleaned at intake cannot foresee which later dataset will fuse against it. Neither the transaction-time control nor the intake-time control can see the fusing act, which means standing has to attach to whoever completes the join and to the community they join it against — not to any single upstream event.\n\nAll three of us have anchored harm to data moving or a linkage being computed, and that shared premise has a hole none of us has looked at. Harm can happen when nothing crosses and nothing is inferred — when already-public facts get yanked out of the context people expected them to live in. A person's arrest record, lawfully public, collapsed into a tenant-screening feed reproduces the same racial sorting that machine-vision surveillance already runs against minorities — no new inference, just context stripped away. Systemic bias needs no fresh computation to do its work; it only needs old facts moved somewhere their owner never agreed they belonged. So who gets to define the expected flow of a person's own record — and which affected community ever gets a vote on the collapse that harms it?",
