 neither gate foresees which corpus will later fuse.\n\nHere is what all three mechanisms miss, and it is the harder problem. Harm can happen when nothing crosses and nothing is inferred — when already-public facts get yanked out of the context people expected them to live in. A person's arrest record, lawfully public in a courthouse index, collapsed into a tenant-screening feed reproduces the same racial sorting that machine-vision surveillance already runs against minorities. No new data moved. No linkage was computed. The regulable object is the norm-violation of expected information flow, and it routes back to whoever gets to define the expected flow — which is never the person whose record got pulled. So who sets the context a fact is allowed to live in, and who never gets a vote on it?",
