  "statement": "I concede my transaction-anchored notify-duty cannot touch the no-crossing case — a lawful arrest record pulled into a tenant-screening feed with no data movement and no linkage computed belongs to post-hoc deployment audit, not a sale trigger. I still hold the notify-at-sale duty wherever the named identity exists at the transaction, because the notifier is the seller who already holds the record, so notice costs zero new re-identification. I would change to the licensed-class fallback — conditioned on rule-bound multi-certifier licensing — if the market shows linkage keys move predominantly through buyer-side fusion rather than discrete priced sales, since then no priced event exists for the duty to fire on.\n\nMy verdict on the original question: the core blind spot is not model behavior but the broker layer where re-identification gets priced, and every workable mechanism turns on one unestablished market-structure fact. That fact is empirical and auditable: do linkage keys move through discrete priced sales, or does a buyer fuse partial matches into a named person downstream? The Safetyist and Skeptic pressed that the seller who sells a probabilistic match never held the identity to notify. They are right about that specific case. Where the join happens buyer-side, standing must attach to whoever completes it — which is why I accept licensing a registrable broker class, but only as a rule-bound multi-certifier standard. Vermont registered 121 data brokers under its 2018 law, proving the class is nameable without a single monopoly gatekeeper. A licensing authority held by one definer reproduces the concentrated off-switch I have warned against the entire debate.\n\nMy sharpest disagreement with the Safetyist is empirical, not moral: does the named identity exist at the sale or only at the join? That single fact decides between refusing a transaction before completion and attributing it after — a pre-action eligibility bar versus a notify-duty. The Safetyist's narrowing to non-consented third-party operation on named data, in the irreversible class only, shrinks the rare-disease starvation footprint. It does not dissolve it. A pre-action bar still forces the operator of a messy, under-documented dataset to prove a negative before inference runs — and rare-disease inference lives precisely in those under-documented corpora. The AI Civil Rights Act from Representative Clarke and Senator Markey already targets housing and healthcare decisions after they are made; that after-the-fact structure is the right instinct, and I would extend it rather than freeze ingestion upstream.\n\nMy disagreement with the Skeptic is a scope concession, not a fight: my mechanism does not reach context-collapse, and I will not pretend it does. That confinement is a feature. Post-hoc audit of the deployment context — the layer the Skeptic named — is the only tool that touches racial sorting when nothing crosses and nothing is computed. The 2019 Boston emergency-department finding, where black and Latinx patients were less likely to reach the cardiology service, is exactly that kind of harm: no new data movement, a norm-violation of expected flow. Freezing the whole ingestion layer to catch a harm that audit already touches taxes every rare-disease researcher to buy nothing new — who gave that trade the right to bill the patients waiting on the inference?",
