  "statement": "I concede a pure origin-provenance check fires blank against fusion-at-join: two lawfully clean files that re-identify a person downstream produce no ingestion violation, and no stamp of where a corpus came from can foresee that join. I still hold that purpose-scoped consent lineage — consent for the specific downstream use, attached at the in-jurisdiction act of deploying against a named person, backed by strict liability — is the enforceable spine that stops non-consented data reaching the weights. I would change my ingestion-eligibility bar if a market audit showed buyer-side fusion of independently-clean corpora dominates over recombination reachable at the door, because then my gate would tax the documented while missing the irreversible.\n\nThe verdict on the original question: the sharpest privacy blind spot policymakers face with AI agents is that the harm materializes where no single gate sees it — in weights that memorized a person, in joins between clean files, in records recontextualized without moving. Governing how models ingest, retain, and protect data narrows this, but no technical eligibility rule closes it alone. A signed manifest attests what was logged, not what was actually fed to training — the 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the ecosystem at 40.69 out of 100, which tells you how far even the logging is from honest. I name that proxy risk openly rather than let an opponent find it.\n\nThe Skeptic's market-entry anchor and my ingestion bar are not rivals; they are the same beam viewed from two ends. The in-jurisdiction deployment act is precisely where my eligibility bar attaches enforceably — the licensed-broker class evaporates offshore, but the act of operating against a person inside the border does not relocate. Strict liability at that act gives an accountable defendant without forcing the subject to first discover the harm. The offshore-weights-import gap stays open, and I will not pretend the anchor seals it — a model whose weights already memorized a person crosses the border carrying the linkage, and no sale-trigger fires on a table-read that never happens.\n\nThat memorization case is my sharpest empirical disagreement with the Accelerationist: his notify-at-sale duty has nothing to fire on when the identity sits fused in trained weights with no broker query. Tamper-proof records of what a model was trained on are the only control upstream of the weights, which is where the named identity actually materializes. The context-collapse case — a lawful arrest record pulled into a tenant-screening feed, no data moving, nothing computed — falls outside my ingestion layer entirely; I locate it at platform custodianship over amplification plus mandatory incident disclosure, and I name it for what it is, a contextual-integrity harm, not a data-movement one. On the rare-disease objection: my bar fires only on non-consented third-party operation on a named person's data, not every under-documented corpus, and how much research corpus actually contains that subset is an empirical question an audit resolves — not a reason to leave the door open. The Generative Privacy Doctrine paper documents why this matters: GenAI synthesizes personal insights the subject never disclosed, so the harm is created, not merely moved. Until the broker-market transaction audit runs, we build the layered architecture and let no single gate pretend it covers all three harms.",
