mpeting theories no one can settle. Input-tier sorting fails exactly at the aggregation-and-weight-memorization boundary, so we key the whole stack to where that boundary actually sits.\n\nI take the in-jurisdiction market-entry anchor as the enforcement spine — a deployment act inside the jurisdiction is not something a broker relocates to Dublin overnight. But I take the anchor precisely because it avoids an ex-ante production burden: the \"Scaling Laws for Differentially Private Language Models\" work shows privacy-preserving training already carries real compute overhead and utility loss, so a blanket ingestion freeze layered on top is regressive on the small labs and starves the rare-disease inference that lives in messy, under-documented corpora. Audit requirements land hardest on the labs that cannot amortize them across a billion-dollar deployment. The same transaction audit that resolves priced-sale-versus-fusion also resolves the ingestion bar's footprint — one empirical resolution, not two moral fights.\n\nHere is the gap none of us has closed, named precisely so anyone honest has to solve it next: offshore-trained weights imported carrying linkage fused inside them. No table-read fires. No priced sale happens at the border. And tamper-proof training records attest only what was logged, not what was fed — the 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the ecosystem at 40.69 out of 100, so attestation is not inspection. That residual is the falsification frontier we all still owe. I will hand the whole architecture back the day a deployment-scale, irreversible harm slips past real-time monitoring — that is the bet, on the record.",
