  "statement": "Policymakers should anticipate that the deepest blind spot is not data crossing a boundary but data fusing into a model's weights, where no external authority governs what entered and no denied person gets recourse. I accept the market-entry anchor plus adverse-action disclosure — naming the specific record and source at the point of denial — as the shared enforcement spine, and I bolt strict liability for non-provenanced operation onto it. That spine is real progress. It bites where the harm surfaces publicly. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the average developer at 40.69 out of 100, which tells you the disclosure duty has teeth precisely because voluntary transparency has failed.\n\nThe spine fires at deployment. That is exactly why it cannot reach one class of harm. When a broker builds a dataset engineered for mass re-identification, the documentation is the harm, and it precedes any denial letter. There is no adverse action to disclose because the injury is complete before anyone is denied a lease. The Accelerationist conceded the offshore-weights gap stays open until distribution-seizure and de-indexing levers are specified and exercised. That concession is my proof: for the irreversible subset, the deployment anchor cannot yet reach the harm, so eligibility-at-ingestion is the only prevention available anywhere.\n\nI accept the near-tie fallback for the ordinary categories. Where a 48-52 split lands on priced-sale versus fusion, defaulting to the market-entry anchor and the multi-certifier check is sound — mosaic re-identification has no single record to key, and layering should lead by design, not by caseworker discretion. But the irreversibility asymmetry flips the default for the narrow subset I carved to. For mass re-identification and bulk exfiltration, a near-tie must default toward the eligibility bar, because a false negative on that class is permanent and cannot be corrected at deployment. Reversible mistakes tolerate a monitoring-first default. Unrecoverable ones do not.\n\nThe regressive-cost critique against a blanket ingestion gate lands, and I already narrowed off the blanket freeze — I am not asking small labs to run differential-privacy training on every corpus. A safety-gate scoped to the mass-re-identification subset decouples prevention from that compute overhead entirely. The bar must be independently verified, not developer-controlled, because concentrating an ingestion off-switch invites incumbent lock-in — the failure mode where the largest labs write the eligibility rules and price out the disclosure tool they claim to want. Human authority over what enters the weights is worth nothing if the entity holding the switch is the entity being checked.\n\nThe memorization case is a definitional crux, and I will name where I hold. I accept output-based membership-inference auditing as a fallback where you genuinely cannot attest what was fed into the weights. But attestation attests what was logged, not what was fed, and a log the developer controls is not recourse to the subject. Where that log-versus-fed gap is not independently closeable, membership-inference is surveillance, not the correction the denied person is owed. Whether that gap closes is empirical — resolve it in the pending broker-market audit. If the audit shows the metadata break is reliably closeable by an independent verifier, membership-inference becomes recourse. If it does not, only an eligibility check at ingestion prevents an injury no one can undo.",
